course: biology
lesson: 149
title: Myth check: are elephants scared of mice?
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In cartoons, a huge elephant screams and leaps onto a stool at the sight of a tiny mouse; in Dumbo, a whole row of elephant ladies panics at Timothy the mouse.
explain: There is no good evidence that elephants fear mice in particular. Elephants see poorly and can be startled by anything small that darts suddenly near their feet: a mouse, a bird or a plastic bag blowing past.
explain: Mice do not crawl up elephant trunks either: the trunk is a mighty muscular nose that blows dust and water over the elephant's back and could snort out any intruder.
explain: What elephants really fear is the buzzing of angry bees, which can sting the soft skin inside the trunk and round the eyes. In Kenya, farmers hang beehives along fences, and the elephants keep away from their fields.
fact: There is no good evidence that elephants fear mice in particular; a TV test once showed an elephant stepping back from a mouse, but one test is not proof.
fact: Elephants see poorly and are startled by sudden small movements near their feet, whether a mouse, a bird or a plastic bag; horses shy at bags too.
fact: Mice do not crawl up elephant trunks; the trunk's strong muscles could blow anything out with a huge snort.
fact: Elephants really avoid bees: at the buzz of an angry swarm they shake their heads, walk away and warn each other with a special rumble.
fact: Bees can sting the soft skin inside the trunk and round the eyes, and in Kenya beehive fences keep elephants out of fields while giving honey.
fact: Elephants feel deep rumbles from far-off elephants through their feet and trunks, and make many sounds too low for people to hear.
fact: Elephants have excellent memories: old female leaders remember distant water in droughts and recognise friends after many years.
fact: Elephants investigate new things carefully with their trunks and can smell water several kilometres away.
fact: An elephant spreads its ears to look bigger and charges to scare a threat, but many charges are bluffs that stop before contact; flapping ears also cools it.
fact: Some farmers smear fences with chilli and grease or burn chilli bricks, because chilli stings elephants' sensitive trunks.
fact: Baby elephants stay close to mothers and aunts, who circle them when lions or hyenas come; babies suck their trunks for comfort.
fact: Ants living inside the thorns of some acacia trees swarm and bite the soft inside of an elephant's trunk, so elephants leave those trees alone.
exercise: Is there good evidence that elephants fear mice in particular? | No, they are startled by any sudden small movement | Yes, elephants panic at the smell of mice | Yes, scientists proved it in many careful tests with real mice | No, elephants have never met a mouse
exercise: Do mice crawl up elephants' trunks? | No, a trunk could snort out anything with great force | Yes, mice often hide inside trunks | Yes, but only at night, when the elephants are fast asleep in the grass | No, because mice are scared of the dark
exercise: What do elephants really run from? | The buzzing of angry bees | The squeak of a mouse | The smell of a cat | The sound of rain
exercise: Why do elephants fear bees? | Bees sting the soft skin in the trunk and round the eyes | Bees eat the elephants' food | Bees carry mice on their backs | Bees can sting right through the thick elephant skin anywhere on its body
exercise: Why do Kenyan farmers hang beehives on their fences? | To keep elephants out of their fields | To feed honey to the elephants as a treat | To catch the mice that eat their maize | To keep the bees well away from the people who live in the village
exercise: An elephant stands still with its trunk on the ground. What is it doing? | Feeling deep rumbles from far-off elephants | Smelling for juicy roots buried deep under the soil | Resting its heavy trunk on the ground for a while | Looking for a mouse hiding in the tall dry grass
exercise: Is it true that «an elephant never forgets»? | Elephants have excellent memories for places and friends | No, elephants forget everything in a day | Yes, they remember every single thing that ever happens to them, perfectly | No, only baby elephants can remember
exercise: How did old female elephants help their families in a drought? | They remembered where distant water was | They dug deep wells with their long tusks | They carried water in their big flapping ears | They called the rain clouds over with their low rumbles
exercise: How does an elephant check a strange new object? | It sniffs and touches it carefully with its trunk | It kicks it straight away | It ignores it completely | It stares at it closely with its very sharp eyes to see what it is
exercise: An elephant spreads its ears wide and charges. What often happens? | It stops before contact; it was a bluff | It always hits whatever is in front | It runs past and keeps going | It falls asleep after the charge
exercise: Why do chilli fences keep elephants away? | Chilli stings their sensitive trunks | Elephants hate the colour red | Chilli makes the ground slippery | Chilli attracts bees to the fence
exercise: Why does a baby elephant hide under its mother? | The herd protects it from lions and hyenas | It is cold and wants to sleep in the warm middle | It is hiding from the mice running in the grass | It is thirsty and trying to drink water from its mother's trunk
exercise: Why might an elephant spook at a plastic bag blowing past? | Sudden, unknown movement near the feet could be danger | Elephants hate the smell of plastic | Bags look like bees to them | It thinks the bag is a mouse every time and is too scared to go near
exercise: How do ants protect some acacia trees from elephants? | They swarm and bite inside the elephant's trunk | They eat the elephants' food first | They make all the leaves on the tree taste hot, like chilli peppers | They build fences round the trees
exercise: How do elephants talk to each other over long distances? | With deep rumbles, many too low for people to hear | With loud squeaks like mice | With bright flashes of their big ears that others see from far away | Only by touching trunks
test: «3 elephants at the waterhole, how many ears and trunks altogether» | 9 | 6 | 3 | 12
test: In Dumbo, the elephant ladies shriek and climb on each other when Timothy the mouse appears. What is real? | Elephants may startle at any sudden small thing; there is no good evidence of a special fear of mice | Real elephants faint at the sight of mice, which is why zoos keep mice out of their enclosures | Real elephants climb trees to escape mice | Real elephants chase mice to eat them
test: «saw it on telly, elephant backed off from a mouse, so it's proven» What is right? | One test is not proof; it may just have been startled by the movement | Yes, one test proves it forever | Yes, and all elephants do it | No, the elephant was really scared of the camera and the people filming it
test: What would happen if a farmer replaced her beehive fence with a fence of pet mice? | The elephants would probably walk right in | The elephants would run away screaming | The mice would chase the elephants | The elephants would bring honey
test: «why'd the herd suddenly turn and leave, there was just some buzzing» What is the most likely reason? | They heard angry bees and warned each other with a rumble | They heard a mouse and panicked | They heard the farmer's radio | They heard rain coming
test: «elephant's charging, ears out, we're dead, run!!» What is useful to know? | Many charges are bluffs, and spreading ears makes it look bigger | Elephants charge only when they are happy and want to play with you | Spreading ears means it is about to sleep | Elephants never charge at anything
test: «4 elephant families, 2 babies each, babies hide under mums, how many babies hiding» | 8 | 6 | 4 | 16
test: A mouse crawls into a sleeping elephant's trunk in a cartoon and the elephant goes mad. What would really happen? | The elephant would blow it out with one huge snort | The mouse would live happily in the trunk for years | The elephant could never breathe through its trunk again | The mouse would climb up the trunk into the elephant's head and stay there
test: «just a stupid bag, why'd the elephant freak out» Why might any big animal shy at a bag? | A sudden strange movement near the feet could be a danger | Elephants want to eat plastic | The bag smelled of bees | Elephants hate the colour white
test: «that baby elephant sucks its trunk like my little bro sucks his thumb» Is that real? | Yes, baby elephants suck their trunks for comfort | No, elephants cannot bend their trunks that far round | No, it is trying to blow its nose after a dusty walk | Yes, but only when it sees a mouse and wants to feel safe again
test: Why do elephants leave some acacia trees alone even when they are hungry? | Ants inside the thorns bite the soft inside of their trunks | The trees have no leaves | The trees are too tall for them | Mice live in those trees
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the alleged fear of mice in elephants. | No sound evidence exists of a particular fear; elephants are startled by any sudden small movement near the feet | It is established beyond doubt that elephants fear mice above all other creatures, having been bitten by them | Elephants are attracted to mice and pursue them | Elephants are incapable of perceiving mice
exam: Identify the principal reason for which elephants avoid the swarming of bees. | Bees are able to sting the soft skin within the trunk and about the eyes | Bees consume the vegetation upon which elephants feed | Bees convey mice upon their bodies | Bees are able to penetrate the thick skin upon any part of the elephant's body
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the charge of an elephant with ears extended? | It is frequently a display which terminates before contact | It invariably concludes in physical contact | It is an expression of contentment | It precedes a period of sleep
exam: Ascertain the means by which certain acacia trees are protected against browsing elephants. | Ants inhabiting the thorns attack the soft interior of the trunk | Mice inhabiting the branches frighten the elephants | The trees emit a smoke of chilli | The trees are surrounded by tall fences erected around them by the ants
